{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC-3 CROSS-CHECK — observed accounting vs the documented contract in docs/llm-framework.md\nsection \"The structured retry layers and their accounting (one bounded operation)\", count by\ncount, over the four live calls above (2 Anthropic + 2 Bedrock).\n\n1. \"exactly one outer Agent.run_sync per output mode\" / \"exactly one on a well-formed first\n   response\" — MATCHES. requests = 1 on all four calls, finish_reason = stop, typed\n   completion_verdict returned each time. No call recorded a second model request.\n\n2. Output-repair allowance N = min(OUTPUT_RETRIES, max(0, structured_retry_limit)) —\n   MATCHES as to the value. output_retry_allowance resolved to 2 on all four calls (the\n   documented default OUTPUT_RETRIES, with structured_retry_limit unset). Zero of that\n   allowance was consumed, consistent with requests = 1.\n\n3. Transport retry \"does not add a model request to the usage tally\" — NOT EXERCISED. No\n   HTTP-layer failure occurred, so no transport resend happened. Recorded as unexercised\n   rather than claimed as confirmed.\n\n4. native->prompted downgrade is \"the only sanctioned second outer run_sync\" — NOT TRIGGERED,\n   and consistently so: native structured output is supported on both models, no grammar\n   rejection occurred, and requests = 1 rules out a second outer run.\n\n5. \"Complete-or-omit history ... an over-budget failed response is omitted whole, never\n   truncated\" — the COMPLETE branch MATCHES and is directly observed (see the two evidence\n   comments: 1 message in, 1 kept, 0 omitted, on every request). The OMIT branch is\n   structurally unreachable on a well-formed first response, because it only applies to a\n   failed intermediate output response, of which there were none. Unexercised, not divergent.\n\n6. \"Trace, not usage, for candidates\" — NOT EXERCISED. Each canary arm ran a single-candidate\n   model, so no fallback candidate was attempted and discarded; there was nothing for the\n   trace to hold apart from usage.\n\n7. CACHE READ / WRITE / INPUT relationship — MATCHES, identically on both providers, and\n   confirms the inclusive-input convention rebar documents (docs/ci-provider-matrix.md\n   footnote 1: input_tokens as logged is inclusive of cache read and cache write, so billable\n   uncached input is input - cache_read - cache_write):\n     Anthropic call 1: 10759 - 0     - 10697 = 62 uncached\n     Anthropic call 2: 10759 - 10697 - 0     = 62 uncached\n     Bedrock   call 1: 10747 - 0     - 10685 = 62 uncached\n     Bedrock   call 2: 10747 - 10685 - 0     = 62 uncached\n   All four resolve to the same 62 uncached input tokens — the non-cached user turn — with the\n   byte-identical prefix moving cleanly from the write column to the read column between call\n   1 and call 2. The two providers differ by 12 tokens on the same prefix (10759 vs 10747),\n   i.e. tokenizer/serialization overhead, not an accounting discrepancy.\n\nVERDICT: on every count the happy path can exercise, the observed accounting matches the\ndocumented contract. No divergence was found in the canary runs themselves.\n\nONE DIVERGENCE FOUND ELSEWHERE, filed rather than reconciled: this session's own plan-review\ngate run on this ticket recorded 32 LLM calls, 785,178 input tokens, cache_read_tokens = 0 and\ncache_write_tokens = 0, with provider provenance prompt_cache_style = \"none\" over the candidate\nchain [\"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\"]. That zeroing\nis deliberate and already explained in rebar.toml and in runner_support._intersect_capabilities\n(a mixed-provider chain collapses to \"none\" because the two providers' cache keys are not\ninterchangeable) — it is NOT a bug and NOT a contradiction of the canary results. But the\naccounting section of docs/llm-framework.md, which is the section this criterion cross-checks\nagainst, does not mention it, so an operator reconciling a real 0/0 gate run against that\nsection has no explanation there. Filed as a documentation follow-up, linked discovered_from\nthis ticket."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787184885122583001,"uuid":"65b4d959-a021-47f9-8ab9-f3f10048f587"}